{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And on the seventh day there shall be for you holiness, a sabbath of complete rest.</b> We do not find that Moses warned concerning the Sabbath here as he was commanded in the preceding section, to say to them ״ושמרתם את השבת״ — 'and you shall keep the Sabbath' — ״אך את שבתותי תשמורו״ — 'but you shall keep My sabbaths.' Therefore it seems to me that it is alluded to us here that one must not reveal hidden matters to the multitudes, and therefore he mentioned above ״אך את שבתותי תשמורו״ — which is an allusion to זכור (remember) and שמור (keep), and he said ״ושמרתם את השבת״ and did not say את יום השבת (the day of the Sabbath), because it refers to the supernal Sabbath; and likewise ״ושמרו בני ישראל את השבת לעשות את השבת״ — as I hinted there. And these hidden matters included in the commandment of the Sabbath, Moses was commanded by the Holy One, blessed be He, to reveal only to the elite of Israel, and therefore there he mentioned ״דבר אל בני ישראל״ — that he should make known to them the matter of the Sabbath in what is revealed and in what is hidden. But here, Moses speaks to the multitude — to all the congregation of Israel, the men and the women — and therefore he did not mention the allusions to the supernal Sabbath or anything of what is hidden in it, and did not wish to speak to them except in the plain sense of the commandment, in what is revealed about the Sabbath alone, and in brief. And so Moses has opened for us a door through his words to conceal the secrets of the Torah. And there is further proof from what they expounded in tractate Megillah concerning Onkelos: when he translated the Torah, a bat kol went forth and said, 'Who is he that has revealed secrets to the children of men?' — and if regarding the translation of the Torah it is so, then all the more so (קל וחומר) for one who elaborates further in explaining the secrets of the Torah before the multitude, who is called a revealer of secrets and a talebearer who reveals a secret." "<b>Whoever does work on it shall be put to death.</b> And adjacent to it: ״לא תבערו אש בכל מושבותיכם״ — 'you shall not kindle fire in all your dwellings.' It can be interpreted that these verses come to warn concerning work and to forbid it on the Sabbath: he said first ״כל העושה בו מלאכה יומת״ — that is, the work of the Tabernacle, and concerning this he did not say כל מלאכה (all work), as is the manner of the Torah and the verses concerning the Sabbath. And afterward he mentioned and warned for all generations: ״לא תבערו אש בכל מושבותיכם״ on the day of the Sabbath — and it is as if he said: you shall not do any work in all your dwellings, and he included all labors under the labor of fire, for it is well known that most of the labors in which people engage are done by means of fire, for fire is their cause and their principle. And therefore our Rabbis of blessed memory established for us in the commandment of Havdalah at the conclusion of the Sabbath — which is the beginning of the permissibility of labors — to bless over fire: ״בורא מאורי האש״ (who creates the lights of the fire), because fire is the beginning of labors in the work of Creation, as it is written on the first day: ״ויהי אור״. And behold, the four blessings of Havdalah are inscribed there for every discerning person: the blessing over wine is alluded to in the verse of Genesis in the word הארץ (the earth), which is the garden, and the vine is in the garden, and it is the wine preserved in its grapes since the six days of Creation. The blessing over fragrant woods is alluded to in the word ״ורוח אלהים״ (and the spirit/wind of Elohim), for scent comes by means of the רוח — that is, the air that a person inhales and draws into his nostrils; and further, from what is known that the winds carry the scent. The blessing over the lights of fire is expressed in ״יהי אור״ (let there be light). The blessing of the One who distinguishes between sacred and profane — as it is said: ״ויבדל אלהים בין האור ובין החשך״ (and Elohim separated between the light and the darkness). And understand this."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:07:31.019428125Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}